","order":2,"group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"lastValue":"I'll execute the gap analysis now. Starting with data load and proceeding through all phases silently.Good. `gap_analysis.json` is empty (re-invocation guard passes). All required files loaded successfully. Proceeding with requirement extraction and analysis.\n\nThe candidate has:\n- Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe (completed 2023) — matches required qualification\n- Expired First Aid (2022)\n- Retail work history (Bunnings 2024–present, Coles 2020–2024) — no laboratory experience\n- Basic technical skills (Microsoft Word, POS)\n- No laboratory-specific technical skills, LIMS experience, or analyser experience\n\nNow building the full requirements array, matching against candidate profile, and constructing strengths/gaps.The validator tool timed out. Proceeding to read the verdict file directly.The validator has issued a REJECT verdict with 4 blocking issues. All four concern false-positive strengths — curriculum-inferred or assumption-based claims not explicitly documented in the CV.\n\nRe-reading the candidate profile to confirm:\n\nThe CV contains:\n- Education: Bachelor of Biomedical Science (La Trobe University, completed 2023)\n- Work history: Bunnings (Team Member, 2024-present), Coles (Checkout Operator, 2020-2024)\n- No explicit mention of: pipetting, specimen handling, laboratory safety, quality assurance/control, medical terminology, biochemistry test panels, or shift patterns (weekend/public holiday)\n\nThe validator is correct. The curriculum-implied exception was over-applied. These are substantive knowledge/skill claims that require explicit demonstration in the CV, not curriculum inference alone.\n\nApplying fixes: removing four strengths (strength_2, strength_3, strength_4, strength_5 → IDs strength_2, strength_3, strength_4, strength_5) and creating corresponding gaps.# ✓ Analyst Complete\n\n4 strengths, 10 gaps identified.\n\nTop strength: Bachelor of Science (Biomedical Science, Biochemistry, Medical Laboratory Science, or closely related discipline) from a recognised Australian or equivalent institution\n\nKey gap: Demonstrated understanding of basic laboratory practices, including pipetting techniques, specimen handling, and laboratory safety (severity: High)"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"dropdown","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":3612,"currentPath":["{#}j4QfvI8:3445","{#}gkdsvjW","{#}jAJkqFy","{#}vUHLRbu","{#}wFLxPRd","{#}TCHSV7e","{#}U51BUs0","{#}pXRUHZo","{#}RnVBKab","{#}c3370Ny"]},"helpText":"The name of the agent that will process chat interactions","controlSettings":{"options":["Interviewer","Reviewer","Extractor","ProjectSetup","JD Enhancer","Analyst","Tone Analyst","Assembly Coordinator","Main Orchestrator","Style Reviewer","Style Negotiator","Skills Curator","Profile Builder","Integrity Checker","Credentials Formatter","Cover Letter Writer","History Formatter","Researcher","Document Formatter","Validator"],"group":null},"name":"AgentSelector","order":0,"group":null,"lastEventId":null,"lastValue":"Main Orchestrator"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.pb_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":14,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sc_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":17,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.hf_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":8,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.cf_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":5,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.clw_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":6,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2081,"currentPath":["{#}Gd9f2Xf","{#}utGzLfq","{#}2FKFsy9","{#}utGzLfq","{#}UVjYpRT","{#}opQAm1O"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.extractor_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":7,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.researcher_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":15,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.jde_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":11,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4465,"currentPath":["{#}C6VP:3890","{#}a9ap","{#}754dTKM"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ta_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":20,"lastValue":"Starting Phase 1 — loading all source documents in parallel.All four source files loaded. Proceeding through Phases 2–6 silently.File confirmed written. Now calling the tone_validator.The validator sub-agent timed out. Attempting one retry before proceeding.The `tone_validator` timed out on both attempts (120 s each). Per protocol the retry is the limit — proceeding to Phase 8.\n\n---\n\n# ✓ Tone Analyst Complete\nStyle analysis complete — 8 issue(s) flagged.\n\npipeline_status: TONE_ANALYZED"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.reviewer_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":16,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ac_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":4,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sn_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":18,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sr_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":19,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4080,"currentPath":["{#}Lwo9k4U:1","{#}EAaQzRk","{#}ItaE4iK"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ic_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":10,"lastValue":"I'll load all required files simultaneously to begin the integrity check.All six files loaded. Now I'll conduct a full, independent semantic-entailment validation across all assembled sections.\n\n---\n\n**Validation working notes:**\n\n**`cv_raw.txt` facts established:**\n- Degree: Bachelor of Biomedical Science, La Trobe University, 2020–2023 ✓\n- VCE, Eltham High School, 2019 ✓\n- Bunnings Warehouse Team Member, Mar 2024–current: helping customers find products; working on register; putting stock away; garden section ✓\n- Coles Checkout Operator, Jan 2020–Feb 2024: scanning groceries; cash handling; sometimes worked in deli ✓\n- Skills stated: Microsoft Word, good at following instructions, team player, reliable ✓\n- First Aid Certificate (expired 2022) — excluded from assembled CV per style override ✓\n- **No lab-specific skills stated anywhere in cv_raw.txt**\n- **No mention of weekends, rotating rosters, or shift patterns**\n\n**`gap_analysis.json` session evidence:** `candidate_provided_evidence` top-level array is empty `[]`. All per-gap adjudications are `\"SKIPPED\"` — **zero ACCEPTED entries.** Gaps confirmed: gap_1 (QC principles), gap_7 (automated analysers), gap_8 (LIMS), gap_9 (NATA), gap_11 (medical terminology / biochem panels).\n\n---\n\n**PROFILE — Track 1 + Track 2:**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"Biomedical Science graduate (La Trobe University, 2023)\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n| \"laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not stated in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence; inferred from degree type = prohibited inference |\n| \"Degree coursework covered biochemistry test panels\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, adjudication SKIPPED |\n| \"quality control principles\" (in degree statement) | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1, SKIPPED |\n| \"medical terminology\" (in degree statement) | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"Four years of daily cash handling at Coles\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt confirms Coles Jan 2020–Feb 2024 + cash handling |\n| \"six-plus years of retail shift work\" | ✅ PASSED — Bunnings ~2yrs + Coles ~4yrs = fair generalization |\n| \"flexibility across rotating rosters, including weekends\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — weekends/rotating rosters not stated in cv_raw.txt; inference from retail type = prohibited |\n\n**SKILLS — Track 1 + gap check:**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"Laboratory pipetting and specimen handling\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"Biosafety and laboratory safety protocols\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"Biochemistry test panels (electrolytes, LFTs…)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"Quality control principles and procedures\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1, SKIPPED |\n| \"Medical terminology\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"LIMS fundamentals\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_8, SKIPPED |\n| \"Automated chemistry analysers (Roche cobas, Abbott Architect)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_7, SKIPPED |\n| \"NATA accreditation standards (foundational)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_9, SKIPPED |\n| \"Data validation and numerical accuracy\" | ✅ PASSED — fair generalization of cash handling / register work |\n| \"Microsoft Word\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not stated in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Attention to detail and accuracy under pressure\" | ✅ PASSED — grounded in cash handling |\n| \"Teamwork and collaboration\" | ✅ PASSED — \"team player\" in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Verbal and written communication\" | ✅ PASSED — fair generalization from customer-facing roles |\n| \"Reliability and follow-through on procedures\" | ✅ PASSED — \"reliable\" in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Adaptability to new systems and protocols\" | ✅ PASSED — generic soft-skill generalization, no specific invented fact |\n| \"Bachelor of Biomedical Science (La Trobe University, 2023)\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n\n**CAREER HISTORY — dual-track per bullet:**\n\nAll Bunnings and Coles employer entries, dates, and responsibilities are confirmed in cv_raw.txt. Rewrites are faithful professional reformulations — \"working on the register\" → cash/EFTPOS transactions; \"garden section mostly\" → Garden section service; \"putting stock away\" → receive and shelve stock; \"Scanning groceries/cash handling\" → transaction processing; \"sometimes worked in deli\" → cross-department deli support. All ✅ PASSED.\n\n**CREDENTIALS:** Bachelor of Biomedical Science ✓, VCE ✓, no certifications (expired credential correctly excluded) ✅ PASSED.\n\n**ADDITIONAL INFORMATION:** No publications, awards, grants, or memberships in candidate_profile.json. Nothing to flag. ✅ PASSED.\n\n**COVER LETTER — candidate-facing claims only (context exemption applied):**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"15,000 specimens / Heidelberg\" | EXEMPT — company fact from research_output |\n| \"My Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe University (2023)\" | ✅ PASSED |\n| \"practical training in pipetting, specimen handling and preparation, and biosafety protocols\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"coursework spanning biochemistry test panels from electrolytes … QC principles\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11 (biochem panels) + gap_1 (QC), both SKIPPED |\n| \"working understanding of QC frameworks: IQC shift checks and Westgard-based result review\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1 + gap_15; specific method names never stated by candidate |\n| \"Four years of daily cash handling at Coles…accurate, protocol-driven data handling\" | ✅ PASSED — grounded in cv_raw.txt |\n| 12-week orientation, AIMS registration, Grade 1 pathway | EXEMPT — company/role framing from research_output |\n| \"Biomedical Science background and track record of precision-focused work\" | ✅ PASSED — generalization of degree + Coles tenure |\n\n---\n\n**Verdict: FAILED — 15 violations (6 × NOT_FOUND + 9 × GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED)**\n\nNow writing the updated state file:## ⚠ Integrity Check Failed\n\n15 issue(s) found — see breakdown below.\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 Remove — no source found in `cv_raw.txt` or session evidence (6)\n\n**Profile (3)**\n1. **[profile]** \"laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety\" — not stated anywhere in the original CV; the degree's curriculum is inferred, not attested by the candidate\n2. **[profile]** \"Degree coursework covered biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, and medical terminology\" — biochem panels (gap_11) and QC principles (gap_1) are registered gaps; session adjudication was SKIPPED; no ACCEPTED evidence exists\n3. **[profile]** \"flexibility across rotating rosters, including weekends\" — weekends and rotating rosters are never mentioned in the original CV; inferred from the fact that retail employers commonly roster weekends — prohibited inference\n\n**Skills (2)**\n4. **[skills]** \"Laboratory pipetting and specimen handling\" — not stated in original CV; no session evidence accepted\n5. **[skills]** \"Biosafety and laboratory safety protocols\" — not stated in original CV; no session evidence accepted\n6. **[skills]** \"Roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" — not stated in original CV; inferred from retail employment type\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 Remove — gap-registered skills with no accepted session evidence (9)\n\n**Skills (5)**\n7. **[skills]** \"Biochemistry test panels (electrolytes, LFTs, renal function, lipids, glucose, cardiac markers, thyroid function)\" — registered gap_11; adjudication: SKIPPED\n8. **[skills]** \"Quality control principles and procedures\" — registered gap_1; adjudication: SKIPPED\n9. **[skills]** \"Medical terminology\" — registered gap_11; adjudication: SKIPPED\n10. **[skills]** \"LIMS fundamentals (Laboratory Information Management Systems)\" — registered gap_8; adjudication: SKIPPED\n11. **[skills]** \"Automated chemistry analysers (Roche cobas, Abbott Architect platforms)\" — registered gap_7; adjudication: SKIPPED\n12. **[skills]** \"NATA accreditation standards (foundational)\" — registered gap_9; adjudication: SKIPPED\n\n**Cover Letter (3)**\n13. **[cover_letter]** \"practical training in pipetting, specimen handling and preparation, and biosafety protocols\" — not stated in original CV; no accepted session evidence\n14. **[cover_letter]** \"coursework spanning biochemistry test panels from electrolytes and liver function through to cardiac markers, thyroid function, and quality control principles\" — gap_11 + gap_1; both SKIPPED\n15. **[cover_letter]** \"working understanding of QC frameworks: the structured precision that IQC shift checks and Westgard-based result review depend on\" — gap_1 + gap_15; SKIPPED; specific method names (IQC, Westgard) never stated by candidate\n\n---\n\n### 📋 What this means for the CV\n\nThe assembled CV drew heavily on **curriculum inference** — assuming that a Biomedical Science degree *must* have covered lab practicals, QC, medical terminology, etc. — and on **gap-filling without session confirmation** (the candidate skipped the gap interview entirely). These are real gaps the candidate hasn't addressed yet.\n\nYou have three paths forward:\n\n- **Type `\"fix N: [your evidence]\"`** — provide your own words confirming a specific claim (e.g., \"fix 1: I did pipetting practicals in my second year Biochemistry unit\")\n- **Type `\"remove N\"`** — authorise removal of a specific claim\n- **Type `\"accept all\"`** — proceed with all 15 claims removed; the CV will reflect only what is in the original document\n- **Click \"Continue anyway\"** — proceed to document formatting without changes (the INTEGRITY_FAILED flag remains on record)\n\n> **Note:** Items 1, 4, 5, 13 (pipetting/specimen handling/biosafety) can be rescued with a single session answer — they all stem from the same gap about your lab practicals. Similarly, items 3 and 6 (roster/weekends) can be fixed if you confirm you worked weekends at Bunnings or Coles.\n\npipeline_status: INTEGRITY_FAILED"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.mo_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":12,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.df_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":21,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":6048,"currentPath":["{#}lWylMGc","{#}utGzLfq","{#}2FKFsy9","{#}tZCB7hx","{#}utGzLfq","{#}nihT30L","{#}wn4M0xy"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.validator_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":22,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":4,"controlType":"anything","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"helpText":"Linked to the 'abort' ports of all agents","controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Settings.Abort_All_Processing","group":"Settings","lastEventId":null,"order":28,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"group","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Agent Debug","order":3,"group":null,"lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"group","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Agent Reasoning","order":2,"group":null,"lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":594,"currentPath":["{#}j4QfvI8:1","{#}gkdsvjW","{#}jAJkqFy"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.analyst_reasoning","order":6,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"The user has requested to start the gap analysis. According to my instructions, I am the Analyst agent and I need to execute the gap analysis process.\n\nLet me review the key execution requirements:\n1. I must ACTUALLY CALL THE TOOLS - not just describe what I would do\n2. Execute all phases with no user-facing output until Phase 9 completes\n3. Reason internally as needed, but only output the completion message at the end\n4. Use bare filenames only - no leading slashes\n\nLet me execute the phases:\n\n**Phase 1: Load Data & Startup Guards**\n\nFirst, I need to read the required files:\n1. Check if `gap_analysis.json` already exists (re-invocation guard)\n2. Read `project_meta.json`\n3. Read `research_output.json`\n4. Read `enhanced_jd.json`\n5. Read `candidate_profile.json`\n\nLet me start by reading these files.","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2238,"currentPath":["{#}4TlnrUw","{#}utGzLfq","{#}2FKFsy9","{#}utGzLfq","{#}gp3IxFM","{#}CcVxC0V"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.jde_reasoning","order":13,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2195,"currentPath":["{#}ifuNjt3","{#}utGzLfq","{#}2FKFsy9","{#}utGzLfq","{#}wSJPCox","{#}bC12hey"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.researcher_reasoning","order":17,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2082,"currentPath":["{#}Gd9f2Xf","{#}utGzLfq","{#}2FKFsy9","{#}utGzLfq","{#}UVjYpRT","{#}opQAm1O"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.extractor_reasoning","order":10,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.mo_reasoning","order":14,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ps_reasoning","order":16,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ac_reasoning","order":5,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.cf_reasoning","order":7,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.clw_reasoning","order":8,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.df_reasoning","order":9,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.hf_reasoning","order":11,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4081,"currentPath":["{#}Lwo9k4U:1","{#}EAaQzRk","{#}ItaE4iK"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ic_reasoning","order":12,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"Let me start by reading all the necessary files to perform the integrity check.Now I have all the files loaded. Let me carefully re-examine the current state.\n\nThe `cv_assembly_state.json` already shows:\n- `current_phase: 9`\n- `metadata.status: \"INTEGRITY_FAILED\"`\n- `phases[7].status: \"COMPLETE\"` (phase_number 8, 0-indexed as phases[7])\n- `phases[7].data` already contains integrity check results with `integrity_status: \"FAILED\"` and 15 unsupported claims\n\nWait, let me look at the structure more carefully. The phases array:\n- phases[0] = phase_number 1 (Style Negotiation)\n- phases[1] = phase_number 2 (Profile Building)\n- phases[2] = phase_number 3 (Skills Curation)\n- phases[3] = phase_number 4 (History Formatting)\n- phases[4] = phase_number 5 (Credentials Formatting)\n- phases[5] = phase_number 6 (Cover Letter Writing)\n- phases[6] = phase_number 7 (Style Review)\n- phases[7] = phase_number 8 (Integrity Check) — already has data\n- phases[8] = phase_number 9 (Document Formatting) — this is where we are now\n\nThe integrity check was already run and failed with 15 unsupported claims, but the user is triggering it again now. Even though the current phase is 9 and the previous integrity check completed, they're re-running the check fresh.\n\nNow I need to validate all the claims in the assembled CV against the raw source data and gap analysis. Looking at the raw CV, I can see Sarah Jenkins' education history from La Trobe University and her work experience at Bunnings Warehouse starting in March 2024.\n\nThe gap analysis reveals a comprehensive list of 19 identified gaps, ranging from quality control principles and MS Office proficiency to lab experience, LIMS systems, and NATA accreditation knowledge. Her First Aid Certificate has lapsed since 2022, and there are significant gaps around medical terminology and biochemistry panels. I need to check how the assembled CV addresses these gaps and whether the candidate evidence provided has been properly adjudicated.\n\nLooking at the profile section, I'm validating the claims made against the raw CV data. The opening statement about being a Biomedical Science graduate from La Trobe University in 2023 checks out against the documented degree completion dates of 2020-2023.\n\nHowever, the claim about laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety is problematic. While the CV confirms the degree itself, it doesn't explicitly state these specific lab skills anywhere. This appears to be an inference about what a typical Biomedical Science degree would include rather than something the candidate actually stated. Under the v3.1 rules, this doesn't qualify as a fair generalization of a stated fact—it's an unstated assumption about the degree's content.\n\nThe gap_analysis.json marks this requirement as \"Met\" based on the analyst's inference that the curriculum covers lab practices, but that assessment doesn't constitute accepted session evidence or direct CV content. The integrity check needs to validate against what's actually documented in cv_raw.txt, not what the analyst inferred about the degree program. The analyst inferred these details rather than drawing them from what the candidate actually stated, which violates the rule against pure inference as evidence. Moving through the remaining claims: biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, and medical terminology all get flagged as fabricated skills since their adjudications were skipped. The cash handling claim at Coles from January 2020 to February 2024 does pass verification with the cv showing over four years in that role, and the requirement for numerical accuracy is a reasonable generalization from that experience.\n\nFor the retail shift work claim, combining Bunnings (roughly two years from March 2024 onward) with the prior Coles tenure gives approximately six years total, which aligns with what's documented. However, the specific assertion about rotating rosters and weekend work isn't explicitly stated in the cv—it's an assumption about what retail work entails rather than something the candidate confirmed. But I'm noticing the gap analysis might have already addressed this requirement elsewhere, so I need to check what evidence was actually recorded there.\n\nMoving into the skills section, I'm finding that several technical competencies like laboratory pipetting, biosafety protocols, and specific biochemistry test panels aren't mentioned anywhere in the raw CV. These appear to be inferences drawn from the candidate's degree rather than demonstrated experience they've actually listed.\n\nThe same pattern continues with quality control principles, medical terminology, LIMS systems, and automated analyzer platforms—all marked as fabricated skills since they were skipped during the analysis. For data validation and numerical accuracy, I'm considering whether cash handling experience from their retail work could reasonably generalize to this skill, though the connection feels like a stretch. NATA accreditation standards is marked as fabricated since it was skipped in the gap assessment. Moving through the soft skills now—attention to detail and accuracy clearly connect to the cash handling work, teamwork is explicitly mentioned as \"team player,\" and verbal and written communication are fair generalizations from customer service roles. Roster flexibility isn't documented anywhere, so that's a gap. Reliability is directly stated in the CV, but adaptability to new systems appears to be cut off mid-evaluation. \"Adaptability to new systems and protocols\" is a soft skill that reasonably follows from working across multiple retail environments, so I'll pass that. But \"roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" is a specific detail that doesn't appear anywhere in the CV—that's fabricated. Moving into the career history section, I'm verifying the Bunnings Warehouse entry: the employer and \"Team Member\" position both check out against the source document. The EFTPOS inference holds up as a reasonable generalization for modern retail. Moving on to verify the Bunnings stock management bullet — shelving work does support inventory accuracy, so that's fair. Now checking the Coles position details, which align with the dates and role provided. Now checking the education credentials — both the Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe in 2023 and VCE from Eltham High School in 2019 check out. Moving into the cover letter, I'm verifying the opening statements about Melbourne Pathology's daily specimen volume, which comes from research and is exempt from the CV, along with the degree reference which is already confirmed. I'm flagging that the practical training details about pipetting and biosafety protocols aren't in the original CV, so I need to investigate further.\n\nIn the connection paragraph, I'm seeing claims about QC frameworks and Westgard-based result review that reference gaps in the CV — specifically gap_1 for QC principles and gap_15 for Westgard methodology. These appear to be fabricated skills not supported by the actual CV content, which is a significant issue.\n\nMoving through the rest of the assessment, the cash handling experience at Coles gets a pass as a fair generalization of protocol-driven accuracy under pressure, and the framing about how laboratory accuracy parallels retail data handling is persuasive context rather than a factual claim, so that passes too. The research paragraph references are exempt since they're drawn directly from Melbourne Pathology's documented orientation program and career pathway details.\n\nNow checking what's left: the candidate profile shows no publications, awards, grants, or certifications to verify, so nothing additional to flag there. But I'm finding some real problems in the degree claims — the statements about laboratory training in pipetting and specimen handling aren't supported in the CV itself, they're inferences from the degree type. Same issue with the claim about biochemistry test panels being covered in coursework, and the quality control principles statement appears to be unsupported as well. Continuing through the remaining skills and cover letter claims — I'm finding more fabricated gaps around automated analyzer platforms and NATA standards, plus additional items marked as inferred from the degree rather than explicitly stated in the CV. I'm seeing the breakdown of individual skill gaps from the profile and skills sections — things like pipetting, specimen handling, biosafety protocols, biochemistry panels, quality control, medical terminology, LIMS fundamentals, and automated analyzer experience are all flagged as either missing or fabricated claims.\n\nThe integrity check confirms the same 15 violations across all sources, so the assessment is consistent. Now I need to update the assembly state to reflect this failed validation. Writing the integrity check output... Continuing through the skills section, marking additional claims as fabricated—quality control procedures, medical terminology, LIMS fundamentals, specific analyzer platforms like Roche and Abbott systems, and NATA accreditation standards all lack supporting evidence in the provided materials. I'm reconsidering whether some of those NOT_FOUND claims could actually qualify under the v3.1 rule about fair generalizations. A Bachelor of Biomedical Science degree would reasonably encompass laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety protocols—that's a fair generalization of what the degree entails, not pure inference. The distinction matters: restating what the candidate actually stated versus inferring something they never mentioned. The Integrity Checker operates independently from the Analyst's assessment, so I'm holding firm on these being NOT_FOUND — the degree is listed but the candidate never explicitly stated those lab skills, and without direct evidence or an accepted session, I can't infer them. Now I'm moving through the career history to verify nothing's been fabricated. The Bunnings register bullet mentions EFTPOS payments, which is a reasonable inference from \"working on the register\" since it's standard in modern retail, though I'm questioning whether naming the specific technology crosses the line into assertion.\n\nI'm settling on FAILED with 15 violations, matching my previous assessment. Now I need to update the state file — I'll do a read-modify-write to update phases[7] with the fresh results and refresh the timestamps. I need to read the existing JSON file and modify it in place rather than reconstructing it from scratch. I'll update the phases[7].data with fresh integrity check results while preserving the current state—keeping the status as \"COMPLETE\", the completed_at timestamp, and the current_phase at 9. Since completed_phases is already 8 from the previous run, I won't increment it again. I just need to refresh the last_updated timestamp to reflect this new check.\n\nNow I'm writing out the updated JSON file and preparing the failure summary with the specific issues found, starting with the missing source reference. I'm seeing nine more fabricated skills spread across the profile, skills section, and cover letter—things like biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, LIMS fundamentals, and specific analyzer platforms that weren't mentioned in the original materials. I'm reviewing the soft skills to verify they're fair generalizations rather than fabrications. \"Data validation and numerical accuracy\" works as a reasonable extension of cash handling experience, and the other soft skills like adaptability, communication, teamwork, attention to detail, and reliability all map back to specific claims in the CV, so I'll pass those through.\n\nThat brings me to a final count of 15 violations total—6 skills marked as not found and 9 that appear to be fabricated gaps. The assessment has failed, so I need to update the state and prepare the output with the corrected data.","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.pb_reasoning","order":15,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.reviewer_reasoning","order":18,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sc_reasoning","order":19,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sn_reasoning","order":20,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sr_reasoning","order":21,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4466,"currentPath":["{#}C6VP:3890","{#}a9ap","{#}754dTKM"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ta_reasoning","order":22,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"The user wants me to run tone validation. I'll start by reading the necessary files - cv_raw.txt, candidate_profile.json, project_meta.json, and attempt to read cover_letter_sample.txt.All four files loaded successfully. Now I'll proceed through the phases silently.\n\n**Phase 2: Seniority Assessment**\n\nTarget: UX/UI Designer — Digital Banking, Sector: Banking / Financial Services\n\nWork history from `candidate_profile.json`:\n1. Bright Pixel Studio — UX Designer (Contract), 2025-03 → Present — **relevant: true, is_employment: true**\n2. Neighbourhood Digital — UX Design Intern (Part-Time), 2024-09 → 2025-02 — **relevant: true, is_employment: true**\n3. Career Break — Primary Carer — 2021-01 → 2024-08 — **relevant: false, is_employment: false**\n4. Ashurst — Senior Associate — Corporate & Commercial, 2015-02 → 2020-12 — **relevant: false, is_employment: true** (legal background, not UX)\n5. Ashurst — Graduate-at-Law / Seasonal Clerk, 2014-02 → 2015-01 — **relevant: false, is_employment: true** (legal background, not UX)\n\nSo the relevant UX experience is just the recent contract role at Bright Pixel Studio spanning a few months.\n\nThere's also an internship at Neighbourhood Digital that lasted around five months, plus some pro bono work during the career break. This is clearly a career-changer from law with only two short UX roles, so she's positioning as a junior UX designer. The bootcamp finished in June 2024, which is about two years out from now, and since she's already landed professional UX work beyond just the bootcamp, I'd mark `is_recent_graduate: false` — she has actual shipped experience even if it's limited.\n\nFor the classification: she's got roughly eight months of combined relevant UX work, so `level: Junior` is right. The current title is UX Designer, and the sector is banking/financial services, which isn't academic or research-focused. That puts her in the `\"direct-practical\"` registration bucket.\n\nLooking at the CV language, the work history uses past tense throughout — even the current role at Bright Pixel Studio is written in past tense with verbs like \"Redesigned,\" \"Led,\" \"Conducted,\" and \"Managed.\" The voice is consistently active across all the bullets. Now I'm examining the bullet format structure. designer on a healthcare patient portal redesign, created wireframes and prototypes in Figma for a nonprofit website migration, and participated in design sprints with client presentations. The bullet points average around 14 words each, with a range from 8 to 22 words depending on the scope and detail of the project.\n\nThe writing style uses action verbs with objects and subordinate clauses, maintaining a semi-formal professional tone without first-person pronouns. The bullets are telegraphic rather than full sentences, with strong quantification in the UX roles and ownership-framed language throughout. The legal experience follows the same crisp register, emphasizing deal values and team management, while the cover letter shifts to a more conversational, narrative-driven voice with self-aware confidence.\n\nI'm noting some weaknesses: the skills section has vague entries and hedging language like \"Comfortable\" and parenthetical qualifications that undercut the otherwise strong ownership framing. The CV demonstrates clear ownership through action verbs like \"Led end-to-end UX\" and \"Managed own client relationships,\" with concrete examples like the council community engagement portal project showing full lifecycle ownership from stakeholder workshops to developer handoff.\n\nThe content leans systems-centric—design artifacts, processes, tools—though it does incorporate people elements. Her signature phrases like \"end-to-end UX,\" the specific metric \"task completion rate from 34% to 71%,\" and \"plain-English\" recur throughout and should be preserved. The cover letter uses an alignment-led persuasion strategy, connecting her non-traditional background directly to role requirements, particularly in financial services where she demonstrates understanding of regulatory constraints and the compliance-usability tension. She employs confident, slightly self-aware statements that make the narrative distinctive, though the thought trails off mid-sentence. Now I'm scanning for weak phrasing and hedging language. \"Background in contract negotiation and client management (transferable from legal career)\" reads defensively with that parenthetical qualifier — it undermines rather than reinforces the point. Similarly, \"Comfortable presenting design rationale\" uses a weak hedge when it should be action-driven, and \"Strong understanding of WCAG 2.1 AA\" is filler that should be backed by concrete work instead of just stated as a skill.\n\nI'm also noticing \"Experienced in translating complex business requirements\" leads with a weak phrasing device; flipping it to lead with the verb would be stronger. The \"working knowledge of HTML/CSS sufficient to collaborate\" construction is similarly defensive with its qualifiers. For the current role at Bright Pixel Studio, the past tense throughout is actually a standard CV convention for completed tasks, so that's not necessarily a problem unless there's inconsistency elsewhere. The skills section leans on \"Comfortable\" as a register choice, which I should flag as part of the overall hedging pattern.\n\nLooking at the real priorities for a UX/UI Designer role at a bank, the CV is solid overall, but I'm spotting two high-priority issues: \"Comfortable presenting design rationale to senior stakeholders\" uses a qualifier that undermines confidence in a professional context, and the parenthetical \"(transferable from legal career)\" in the skills section feels defensive about the career pivot rather than owning those capabilities directly.\n\nBeyond that, there are several medium-priority phrasing issues where weak constructions like \"Experienced in\" and \"Working knowledge... sufficient to\" minimize what should be presented as strengths. The competitive analysis bullet also needs concrete outcomes—right now it just lists an activity without showing what was learned or changed as a result. And the healthcare portal bullet starts passively when it should lead with the designer's actual contribution.\n\nI'm also noticing the skills section uses inconsistent phrasing patterns compared to the experience bullets—noun and adjective leads like \"Comfortable\" and \"Background in\" clash with the verb-forward style elsewhere. Let me pull out the most concrete issues with exact quotes and specific rewording suggestions to make the feedback actionable. direct collaboration with front-end developers. The competitive analysis and heuristic evaluations for those three client projects need concrete outcomes — what insights emerged or what decisions did they inform? Same issue with the healthcare portal work: \"supported\" glosses over the actual contribution, so I should specify what was owned or led. The Education section formatting also needs a look.\n\nThe date formats are all over the place — \"Completed June 2024\" versus \"2011 – 2013\" and \"2007 – 2010\" — so I'll standardize those. I'm also noticing the Skills section header mentions \"Comfortable presenting design rationale,\" which feels tentative compared to the confident tone elsewhere on the CV, though that's already flagged. The cover letter itself reads well with a strong, distinctive voice, so no major issues there.\n\nBut I'm spotting another pattern: \"Familiar with Agile/Scrum workflows\" uses the same weak qualifier I've already called out in other places. This \"Familiar with\" phrasing keeps appearing in the skills section and undermines the overall confidence.\n\nLooking at the severity breakdown now, I have 8 issues total — 2 high severity, 5 medium, and 1 low. That's actually a reasonable distribution for what's otherwise a solid CV. The problems are real but targeted, not systemic like some weaker applications I've seen.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether \"Comfortable presenting\" should stay high severity. For a UX/UI Designer role at a bank, it does undermine credibility in the skills section since that's read first, so I'll keep it flagged as high. The legal career parenthetical is medium — it's an aside that softens the impact but isn't pervasive enough to be critical.\n\nNow I have my final eight issues across high, medium, and low severity, and I'm ready to structure the JSON output with all the findings.\n\nWriting style_findings.json... They completed UX retraining in 2024 and have already delivered contract work, so not a recent graduate, but definitely Junior-level given only about 8 months of relevant UX experience. I'm also noting their writing style tends toward active voice, past tense, verb-led bullets averaging around 14 words, with a semi-formal tone and no first-person pronouns—they favor confident action verbs extended with participial phrases like \"Redesigned X, improving Y.\"\n\nLooking at their profile more closely, the experience section shows strong metric-backed achievements with consistent verb-led structure and solid ownership framing, but the skills section undermines this with hedged language like \"Comfortable,\" \"Working knowledge,\" and \"Familiar with.\" Their cover letter strikes a more conversational, narrative tone that's self-assertively confident. I'm also spotting some unnecessary defensive moves—like the parenthetical about transferable skills from their legal career, and an internship bullet that uses \"Supported\" when they likely contributed more substantially than that verb suggests. weeks\", and I'm also noting that I led the full UX process for a community engagement portal from initial stakeholder input through to handing off to developers. I'm flagging some language issues in the resume — specifically that phrases like \"comfortable\" undermine the confident tone I've established, and I should reframe things like \"presents design rationale to senior stakeholders\" more assertively as someone who actively leads design critiques across teams.\n\nI'm also catching that the defensive parenthetical about the legal career background weakens the claim unnecessarily — the CV already shows that history, so I should just state that I've applied contract negotiation and client management directly in my current UX work. Similarly, \"experienced in\" is too passive compared to the verb-driven language I'm using elsewhere, so I'd strengthen it to \"translates complex business requirements into user flows and wireframes\" and emphasize how I bridge stakeholder needs with design output.\n\nThe hedging language keeps appearing — \"working knowledge\" and \"familiar with\" both minimize what I actually do. For HTML/CSS, I should reframe it as proficiency that enables direct collaboration on responsive implementation rather than just \"sufficient to collaborate.\" And with Agile, I'm not just familiar with it; I actively practice sprint planning and backlog grooming in Jira across my current engagements, so that's the language I need.\n\nI'm also noticing the competitive analysis bullet lacks impact — it doesn't say what I found or how those findings shaped decisions. Same issue with the healthcare portal work where \"supported\" buries my actual contribution. I need to specify what I owned or delivered, not just that I was there helping.\n\nThere's also an inconsistency in how I'm formatting dates across the education section — mixing month-year for the bootcamp with year ranges for degrees. I should standardize that.\n\nNow I'm double-checking these quotes against the source documents to make sure I'm capturing the exact wording. I'm verifying the remaining claims from the CV, checking items 5 through 8 against the source material, and now looking at how the ownership framing is demonstrated through the council community engagement portal project. I need to handle the duplicate Ashurst entries carefully — there are two different roles under the same employer, so I should structure the role classification to distinguish between them by position rather than just keying by employer name alone, otherwise the server matching could cause a mismatch. Now I'll generate the JSON file with both Ashurst entries included.The tone_validator timed out. According to my instructions, I need to handle this case. The validator timed out, which is a tool execution error rather than a REJECT verdict. Since the validator failed to respond, I should proceed to Phase 8 - the retry limit is one re-validation attempt, but since the tool timed out (not a REJECT), I'll note the timeout and proceed to the completion message. Actually, let me re-read the instructions.\n\nThe instructions say:\n- \"If `verdict === \"APPROVE\"`:\" proceed to Phase 8.\n- \"If `verdict === \"FLAG\"`:\" non-blocking, proceed to Phase 8.\n- \"If `verdict === \"REJECT\"`:\" fix and re-validate once.\n\nA timeout is none of these - it's a tool failure. I could attempt once more since this is effectively a failed first attempt, but there's a risk of an infinite loop. The instructions specify calling it once and retrying once on REJECT, but a timeout isn't a REJECT—it's a tool error. I think I should just move forward to Phase 8 rather than risk another timeout.\n\nThe validator timed out, so I can't get a verdict. The file was written and the validator was called, but since it failed to respond, I'll proceed to Phase 8 and note the timeout without adding confusion. The completion message should still be output.The tone_validator timed out twice. Per the protocol, I should proceed after the retry — the retry is the limit. I'll proceed to Phase 8 and output the completion message.\n\nThe instructions say \"Proceed to Phase 8 regardless of the second verdict — the retry is the limit.\"\n\nSo I'll output the completion message now.","lastEventI